<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Vaspurakan</h1><p class="st-meta">908 CE – 1021 CE · capital Van</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Armenian kingdom around Lake Van ruled by the Artsruni dynasty, best known for the sculpted Church of the Holy Cross on Aghtamar island.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Artsruni family had long held the lands south and east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>. In 908 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajid-dynasty">Sajid</a> emir Yusuf crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gagik-i-of-vaspurakan">Gagik Artsruni</a> king as a rival to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smbat-i-of-armenia">Smbat I of Bagratid Armenia</a>, creating a separate kingdom under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sajid-emirate">Sajid Emirate</a> overlordship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gagik-i-of-vaspurakan">Gagik I</a> built a palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathedral-of-the-holy-cross-aghtamar">Church of the Holy Cross</a> on Aghtamar island between 915 and 921, its walls carved with biblical scenes, vines and royal portraits. The kingdom was rich in farmland, fish and trade, and kept a lively literary culture; the History of the House of Artsruni was written there. Its kings alternated between rivalry and alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratids</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Facing Seljuk raids, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senekerim-hovhannes-artsruni">Senekerim-Hovhannes</a> ceded Vaspurakan to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1021 in exchange for estates around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sebasteia</a>, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> followed him west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathedral-of-the-holy-cross-aghtamar">Aghtamar</a> church survives, restored in 2007, as the kingdom's most celebrated monument.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 908 CE – 1021 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.77%;width:2.25%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>908 CE – 1021 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Van</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gagik I Artsruni, Senekerim-Hovhannes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vaspurakan, Artsruni Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=964&amp;focus=kingdom-of-vaspurakan" aria-label="Kingdom of Vaspurakan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5759 795 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Vaspurakan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6079 1051L6069 986M6079 1051L6156 1088M6079 1051L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6069 986h0M6156 1088h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6079 1051h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6079 1051h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Van. <a href="/atlas?year=964&amp;focus=kingdom-of-vaspurakan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
